#!/usr/bin/env bash
# reflex-cadence: 4-59/10 * * * *
# mesh-dram-bw — the node's DRAM BANDWIDTH sense, off the AMD Data Fabric uncore PMU.
#
# The hardware has always had this counter; the driver (`amd_uncore`, in-tree + signed, loads under
# lockdown) was simply never loaded, so /sys/bus/event_source/devices/ held no `amd_df` and the mesh
# had no memory-bandwidth axis at all. This tool is that axis.
#
#   mesh-dram-bw              one-line reading (level + GB/s + % of the node's own derived ceiling)
#   mesh-dram-bw --json       machine-readable
#   mesh-dram-bw --sampler    long-lived 1 Hz appender (the 100%-coverage interval lane; supervised)
#   mesh-dram-bw --test       smoke test: fixtures + a LIVE read + the gate driven RED for real
#
# ── WHAT WAS MEASURED HERE, AND WHAT EACH NUMBER COST (genome@mesh-home 2026-08-16, re-derived
#    independently of the discover find that proposed the axis) ───────────────────────────────────
#
# (1) THE UNIT IS 64 B/BEAT — CALIBRATED TWICE, AND THE FIRST TWO CALIBRATIONS WERE WRONG.
#     `dram_channel_data_controller_N` counts beats, not bytes, and 32 vs 64 B/beat is a 2x error in
#     every downstream number. A "known-size read" is only known if nothing else in the perf window
#     moves DRAM, and twice it did:
#       · mmap(MAP_POPULATE) of a 4 GiB tmpfs file puts the kernel's own prefault work (1M struct-page
#         touches + PTE writes, ~640 MB of traffic) INSIDE the window → 48-52 B/beat, a 25% "error"
#         that is not the unit at all. Fix: setup happens before the window; `perf stat -D <ms>` opens
#         the counters only for the read phase.
#       · a random pointer chase — the textbook "exactly one 64 B line per access" fixture — measures
#         3.8-4.6 beats per access, because prefetch and page-walk traffic ride along on a random
#         pattern. A random-access fixture CANNOT calibrate a bandwidth counter; a long sequential
#         read can, because prefetch overfetch is bounded by the region.
#     Clean differential (read phase only, paired same-duration idle control subtracted, 6+4 reps):
#         4 GiB → 61.9 B/beat · 2 GiB → 65.4 B/beat  (bar was ±15%; 32 B/beat is off by 2x)
#     So: bytes = beats × 64.
#
# (2) THE CHANNEL MAP IS ch0+ch1, AND THE OTHER SIX ARE EXCLUDED BY EVIDENCE, NOT BY ASSUMPTION.
#     perf exposes `dram_channel_data_controller_0..7` on every Zen3, including this 2-channel desktop
#     part (Ryzen 7 5700X, 2 populated DIMMs, one per channel). Measured:
#       · ch0 ≈ ch1 within 1% in every sample, idle and loaded — the two populated channels.
#       · ch3..ch7 read exactly 0 in every sample, idle and loaded.
#       · ch2 is the trap: ~1% of ch0 at idle, so a naive sum quietly adds it. Under a 28 GB/s
#         sequential read it FALLS to 0.05%, and under an 8-way chase to 0.025%. A term that
#         ANTI-correlates with DRAM traffic is not DRAM traffic. Excluded.
#     Second reason, independent of semantics: this DF has 4 counters. Two events fit with zero
#     multiplexing; asking for all eight forces perf to time-share and EXTRAPOLATE — measured
#     `50.00` in the enabled column, i.e. a sum over eight channels is also a sum over half a window.
#
# (3) THE COUNTERS ARE NOT MONOTONIC ACCUMULATORS BETWEEN RUNS — the /proc/pressure `total=` trick
#     does not transfer. A DF counter exists only inside an open perf event: `perf stat` zeroes it at
#     open and frees it at close, and there is no sysfs value to read (amd_df exposes `format/` only).
#     So a stateless CLI cannot delta across runs, and sampling for N seconds every cadence would
#     rebuild exactly mesh-psi's 1.7%-coverage blindness (fe35dd9). The fix is a LIVING reader: the
#     `--sampler` lane holds one perf event open at 1 Hz and appends rows; this tool deltas the
#     SAMPLER LOG across runs (cursor in the state file) for 100% interval coverage plus a peak-1s
#     that a mean would dilute away. Cost of that lane, measured: 0 clock ticks of CPU over 18 s of
#     wall (<10 ms, i.e. <0.06% of one core). Coverage is COMPUTED (rows×period / wallclock), never
#     asserted — a sampler that died mid-interval renders its real percentage, and a stale one
#     renders `iv=na`, never 0.
#
# (4) ARMING, MEASURED — `modprobe amd_uncore` costs 8.6-11.3 ms (5 reps); unload costs ~50 ms. The
#     module is in-tree and signed, so it loads with sig_enforce=Y: no MOK, no reboot. This tool
#     SELF-ARMS at run (idempotent presence check first, so the common path costs nothing) rather
#     than requiring /etc/modules-load.d — self-arming cannot rot into a wired-nowhere declaration,
#     and it leaves the node's boot state untouched (a reboot returns it to as-found). It never
#     unloads: another reader may hold the counter.
#     `perf_event_paranoid=4` on this node means the READ needs root too, so the tool runs
#     `sudo -n perf`; where that is unavailable it exits 2 (honest n/a) instead of guessing.
#
# (5) WHETHER THE AXIS MATTERS — the question the find left open, now answered, and the answer is
#     BOTH: it depends entirely on where the model sits.
#       · qwen3-vl:4b GPU-resident, 98 tok/s → peak 4.1 GB/s over 100 ms = 9.6% of ceiling. DRAM is
#         irrelevant headroom.
#       · the SAME model forced CPU-only (num_gpu:0), 11.3 tok/s → median 31.5 GB/s, peak 39.3 GB/s
#         over 100 ms, sustained 17.7 s = 74% median / 92% peak of the ceiling. DRAM is THE ceiling.
#     Which makes the DIMM finding load-bearing rather than trivia: the parts decode DDR4-3600 and
#     run at 2667 MT/s configured, so a bandwidth-bound CPU inference is leaving ~35% of its token
#     rate in an unset XMP profile.
#
# (6) THE CEILING IS DERIVED PER NODE, NEVER HARDCODED (a constant outlives its reader): configured
#     MT/s × 8 B × populated DIMMs, read from dmidecode and cached 7 days with its derivation. If it
#     cannot be derived, the percentage and the level render `na` — the GB/s still reports.
#
# Honest contract: no amd_df and modprobe fails → exit 2 · perf error or a zero delta → `na` with a
# reason, never 0.00 · sampler stale/absent → `iv=na cov=inst-only`, never a fabricated interval.
set -uo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
[ -n "${HOME:-}" ] || HOME=$(getent passwd "$(id -u)" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f6); export HOME
export LC_ALL=C

MESH="${MESH_DIR:-$HOME/.mesh}"
mkdir -p "$MESH" 2>/dev/null || true
STATE="$MESH/.dram-bw.state"
LOG="$MESH/dram-bw.log"
SAMPLES="${MESH_DRAM_SAMPLES:-$MESH/dram-bw-samples.log}"
PEAKCACHE="$MESH/.dram-bw-peak"
# MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1 diverts every durable write — a --test that stamped the live state/log would
# forge the liveness evidence it exists to check (09f7914).
if [ "${MESH_TEST_SANDBOX:-0}" = 1 ]; then
  STATE="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/.dram-bw.state.sandbox.$$"
  LOG="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/dram-bw.log.sandbox.$$"
  PEAKCACHE="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/.dram-bw-peak.sandbox.$$"
fi

PERF="${MESH_DRAM_PERF:-perf}"
SUDO="${MESH_DRAM_SUDO-sudo -n}"          # settable to empty for a test stub / an already-root caller
SYSFS_DF="${MESH_DRAM_SYSFS:-/sys/bus/event_source/devices/amd_df}"
SAMPLE_SECS="${MESH_DRAM_SAMPLE_SECS:-2}"  # the inst window
SAMPLER_PERIOD="${MESH_DRAM_SAMPLER_PERIOD:-1}"   # seconds per sampler row
SAMPLER_LEASE="${MESH_DRAM_SAMPLER_LEASE:-120}"   # a sampler row older than this = stale, iv=na
BYTES_PER_BEAT=64                          # (1) above — calibrated, not assumed

# THE CHANNEL SET. Two channels, named explicitly, for the reasons in (2). Widening this is a
# semantic claim about the node's UMC map, not a config tweak — --test asserts the width.
DRAM_CHANNELS="${MESH_DRAM_CHANNELS:-0 1}"
ev_list() { local c out=; for c in $DRAM_CHANNELS; do out="$out,amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_$c/"; done; printf '%s' "${out#,}"; }

# ── pure helpers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# fold one axis's two windows, NAMING the winner — a bare max effaces which window saw the traffic,
# and the two answer different questions (a 2 s inst sample catches a spike happening right now; the
# interval mean covers the wallclock the inst sample cannot see). args: inst iv|na → "<val> <winner>"
fold_axis() {
  local a="$1" i="$2"
  [ "$i" = na ] && { printf '%s inst' "$a"; return; }
  [ "$a" = na ] && { printf '%s iv' "$i"; return; }
  awk -v a="$a" -v i="$i" 'BEGIN{ if (i+0 > a+0) printf "%s iv", i; else printf "%s inst", a }'
}

# sum ONLY the declared channels out of a perf -x, CSV. Anything else in the stream is ignored, so a
# stray ch2 row cannot slip into the total.
sum_beats() {   # stdin = perf -x, csv
  awk -F, -v chans="$DRAM_CHANNELS" '
    BEGIN{ n=split(chans,c," "); for(i=1;i<=n;i++) want["amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_" c[i] "/"]=1 }
    { name=$3; if (name in want && $1 ~ /^[0-9]+$/) { t+=$1; seen[name]=1 } }
    END{ k=0; for (s in seen) k++; if (k != n) exit 1; printf "%d\n", t }'
}

# level from % of the node's own derived ceiling. Calibrated against THIS node's measured corpus,
# not an assumed 0..1: idle 1.4-5.9% · single-thread sequential read 67% · CPU-only llama median 74%,
# peak 92%. Re-derive if the corpus moves — these are the current answer to the claim, not the claim.
classify() {
  awk -v p="${1:-}" 'BEGIN{
    if (p == "" || p == "na") { print "UNRATED"; exit }
    if (p >= 75) { print "SATURATED"; exit }
    if (p >= 40) { print "BUSY"; exit }
    if (p >= 10) { print "LIGHT"; exit }
    print "IDLE" }'
}

# ceiling: configured MT/s × 8 B × populated DIMMs, from dmidecode. Cached 7 days WITH its derivation.
derive_peak() {
  local out speed n
  out="$($SUDO dmidecode -t memory 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  speed="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F': *' '/Configured Memory Speed:/ && $2 ~ /^[0-9]+ MT\/s/ {print $2; exit}' | awk '{print $1}')"
  n="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | grep -c 'Configured Memory Speed: [0-9]')"
  [ -n "$speed" ] && [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ] || return 1
  awk -v s="$speed" -v n="$n" 'BEGIN{ printf "%.1f %s %s\n", s*8*n/1000, s, n }'
}
peak_gbps() {
  local cached age
  if [ -r "$PEAKCACHE" ]; then
    age=$(( $(date +%s) - $(stat -c %Y "$PEAKCACHE" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) ))
    cached="$(awk '{print $1}' "$PEAKCACHE" 2>/dev/null)"
    [ "$age" -lt 604800 ] && [ -n "$cached" ] && { printf '%s' "$cached"; return 0; }
  fi
  local d; d="$(derive_peak)" || { printf 'na'; return 1; }
  printf '%s MT/s=%s dimms=%s derived=%s\n' $d "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" > "$PEAKCACHE" 2>/dev/null
  printf '%s' "${d%% *}"
}

# ── arming (4) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
arm() {
  [ -d "$SYSFS_DF" ] && return 0
  $SUDO modprobe amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1
  [ -d "$SYSFS_DF" ]
}

# ── the live read. GATE (c): the module must be loaded AND the counter must produce a NONZERO delta.
# Never "the event is readable" — with amd_uncore unloaded perf does not return 0, it exits 129 with
# "Cannot find PMU `amd_df'", and a leg that swallowed that would render na as a blank row.
sample_gbps() {   # $1 = seconds → echoes GB/s, or fails with a reason on stderr
  local secs="$1" out rc beats
  out="$($SUDO $PERF stat -a -x, -e "$(ev_list)" -- sleep "$secs" 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 0 ] || { echo "perf-rc=$rc" >&2; return 1; }
  beats="$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | sum_beats)" || { echo "channel-missing" >&2; return 1; }
  [ -n "$beats" ] && [ "$beats" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null || { echo "zero-delta" >&2; return 1; }
  awk -v b="$beats" -v s="$secs" -v w="$BYTES_PER_BEAT" 'BEGIN{ printf "%.2f", b*w/s/1e9 }'
}

# ── the interval lane (d): delta the SAMPLER LOG across runs. Coverage is computed, never claimed.
# echoes: "<mean> <peak> <coverage_pct> <rows>" or "na na na 0"
interval_from_sampler() {
  local cursor now
  cursor="$(awk -F'|' '/^cursor=/{print substr($0,8)}' "$STATE" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
  now="$(date +%s)"
  [ -r "$SAMPLES" ] || { echo "na na na 0"; return; }
  # rows are counted PER DISTINCT TIMESTAMP: two samplers appending to one log would otherwise
  # double the row count and mint coverage out of duplicates (the --sampler flock makes that state
  # unreachable, but coverage must not depend on a lock held somewhere else).
  awk -v cur="${cursor:-0}" -v now="$now" -v lease="$SAMPLER_LEASE" -v per="$SAMPLER_PERIOD" '
    $1 ~ /^[0-9]+$/ { last=$1; if ($1 > cur && !($1 in seen)) { seen[$1]=1; n++; s+=$2; if ($2+0 > pk+0) pk=$2 } }
    END{
      if (last == "" || now - last > lease) { print "na na na 0"; exit }   # stale sampler → na, not 0
      if (n == 0 || cur == 0) { print "na na na 0"; exit }                  # no baseline → na
      span = now - cur; if (span <= 0) { print "na na na 0"; exit }
      cov = n * per * 100 / span; if (cov > 100) cov = 100
      printf "%.2f %.2f %.0f %d\n", s/n, pk, cov, n
    }' "$SAMPLES"
}

# ── the sampler (the living reader that makes 100% coverage possible) ────────────────────────────
SAMPLER_CHUNK="${MESH_DRAM_SAMPLER_CHUNK:-3600}"   # perf is re-invoked per chunk: a stranded ROOT perf
SAMPLER_KEEP="${MESH_DRAM_SAMPLER_KEEP:-172800}"  # (whose parent died) then self-terminates in ≤1 h
PERFPID="$MESH/.dram-bw-sampler.perfpid"

# THE ORPHAN REAPER, and it is not defensive decoration — it is the repair for a leak this tool's
# first draft actually produced on this node. `kill <wrapper>` does NOT reap `sudo perf`: the child
# runs as ROOT, so an unprivileged parent cannot signal it, and the awk on the other side of the pipe
# is orphaned too — it keeps reading, so perf never takes the SIGPIPE that "the pipe will reap it"
# assumes. Four root perf sessions accumulated that way in one afternoon, each holding 2 of this DF's
# 4 counters: at two orphans the PMU is full and every later reader gets multiplexed or refused, and
# `modprobe -r amd_uncore` fails "Module is in use" (refcnt 8, no visible user-owned process).
# Called at startup as well as from the trap, because a trap does not run on SIGKILL.
SAMPLER_PAT="perf stat -a -x, -I .* -e amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller"
# A PROBE STRING IS ITSELF A CMDLINE. `sudo -n pgrep -f "$SAMPLER_PAT"` always matches at least one
# process — the `sudo` carrying the pattern in its own argv — so the naive form reports a stranded
# perf that does not exist, and the leak gate it feeds can never go green. Filter on comm.
perf_sessions() {
  local p
  $SUDO pgrep -f "$SAMPLER_PAT" 2>/dev/null | while read -r p; do
    [ "$(ps -o comm= -p "$p" 2>/dev/null)" = perf ] && echo "$p"
  done
}
reap_orphans() {
  local p
  for p in $(perf_sessions); do $SUDO kill -TERM "$p" 2>/dev/null; done
}
AWKPID=""
sampler_cleanup() {
  local sp; sp="$(cat "$PERFPID" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$sp" ] && { $SUDO kill -TERM "$sp" 2>/dev/null; $SUDO pkill -P "$sp" 2>/dev/null; }
  [ -n "$AWKPID" ] && kill "$AWKPID" 2>/dev/null
  reap_orphans
  rm -f "$PERFPID" "$MESH/.dram-bw-sampler.fifo.$$" 2>/dev/null
}
run_sampler() {
  # SINGLE WRITER. Two samplers on one log double every row and burn two more of this DF's four
  # counters (2 sampler + 2 reader is already the whole file — a third opener would multiplex and
  # perf would EXTRAPOLATE the reading instead of measuring it). Nearly created that state by hand.
  exec 9>"$MESH/.dram-bw-sampler.lock"
  flock -n 9 || { echo "dram-bw sampler: another sampler already holds the lock — exiting" >&2; exit 0; }
  arm || { echo "dram-bw sampler: n/a — amd_df absent and modprobe amd_uncore failed" >&2; exit 2; }
  trap 'sampler_cleanup; exit 0' EXIT INT TERM HUP
  reap_orphans      # any perf stranded by a previous sampler's death, before opening our own
  while :; do
    sampler_chunk
    # rotate between chunks — 1 row/s is ~1.7 MB/day; keep 2 days
    if [ "$(wc -l < "$SAMPLES" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" -gt "$SAMPLER_KEEP" ]; then
      tail -n "$SAMPLER_KEEP" "$SAMPLES" > "$SAMPLES.tmp" 2>/dev/null && mv "$SAMPLES.tmp" "$SAMPLES"
    fi
    sleep 1
  done
}
sampler_chunk() {
  local start fifo sp; start="$(date +%s)"
  # A FIFO instead of a `|`, so the ROOT perf's pid is knowable and killable. With a pipeline both
  # members are children of this shell and neither dies when this shell does (see reap_orphans).
  fifo="$MESH/.dram-bw-sampler.fifo.$$"; rm -f "$fifo"; mkfifo "$fifo" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  # perf -I writes interval rows to stderr; stdbuf keeps them flowing.
  $SUDO stdbuf -oL -eL $PERF stat -a -x, -I "$((SAMPLER_PERIOD*1000))" -e "$(ev_list)" -- sleep "$SAMPLER_CHUNK" > "$fifo" 2>&1 &
  sp=$!; echo "$sp" > "$PERFPID"
  stdbuf -oL awk -F, -v start="$start" -v chans="$DRAM_CHANNELS" -v w="$BYTES_PER_BEAT" -v per="$SAMPLER_PERIOD" -v out="$SAMPLES" '
    BEGIN{ n=split(chans,c," "); for(i=1;i<=n;i++) want["amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_" c[i] "/"]=1 }
    $1 ~ /^[ ]*[0-9.]+$/ && ($4 in want) {
      t=$1+0; if (t != cur) { if (k == n) { printf "%d\t%.3f\n", start+cur, acc*w/per/1e9 >> out; fflush(out) } cur=t; acc=0; k=0 }
      acc+=$2; k++
    }
    END{ if (k == n) { printf "%d\t%.3f\n", start+cur, acc*w/per/1e9 >> out; fflush(out) } }' < "$fifo" &
  AWKPID=$!
  # `wait`, not a foreground child: bash defers a trapped signal until the FOREGROUND command
  # returns, so with the awk in the foreground the TERM handler never ran and the cleanup that
  # exists to reap the root perf was itself unreachable. The orphan --test leg caught exactly this.
  # And `wait -n` on BOTH, not just the awk: the fifo's write end is held by `sudo`, not by perf, so
  # a perf that dies alone never gives the awk its EOF — one stuck wrapper sat in do_wait for 5
  # minutes exactly that way, alive to the supervisor, producing nothing. Whichever side goes first,
  # the other is killed and the loop opens a fresh chunk.
  wait -n "$sp" "$AWKPID" 2>/dev/null
  $SUDO kill -TERM "$sp" 2>/dev/null
  kill "$AWKPID" 2>/dev/null
  wait "$sp" "$AWKPID" 2>/dev/null
  AWKPID=""; rm -f "$fifo" "$PERFPID" 2>/dev/null
}

# ── --test ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if [ "${1:-}" = --test ]; then
  if [ -z "${MESH_TEST_TMPD:-}" ]; then
    MESH_TEST_TMPD="$(mktemp -d)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (tmp fence: mktemp -d)"; exit 1; }
    trap 'rm -rf "$MESH_TEST_TMPD"' EXIT INT HUP TERM
    export MESH_TEST_TMPD TMPDIR="$MESH_TEST_TMPD" TMP="$MESH_TEST_TMPD" TEMP="$MESH_TEST_TMPD"
    _rc=0; "$0" "$@" || _rc=$?; exit "$_rc"
  fi
  export MESH_TEST_SANDBOX=1
  _live_state="$HOME/.mesh/.dram-bw.state"; _live_log="$HOME/.mesh/dram-bw.log"
  _sig="$(stat -c '%y %s' "$_live_state" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)|$(stat -c '%y %s' "$_live_log" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)"
  for c in awk perf sudo stat date; do command -v "$c" >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: n/a (no $c on this node)"; exit 2; }; done
  td="$(mktemp -d)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mktemp)"; exit 1; }
  # _sw is the orphan leg's sampler: it MUST die on every exit path, including the FAIL ones. A
  # failing leg once left its own sampler parked in do_wait, where the supervisor read it as UP.
  _sw=""
  # (reap only when THIS test started a sampler — the leg is skipped while production holds the
  #  lock, and an unconditional reap here would kill the live organ's perf.)
  trap 'rm -rf "$td"; [ -n "$_sw" ] && { kill "$_sw" 2>/dev/null; sleep 1; reap_orphans; }' EXIT

  # 1. fold_axis — the max that names its winner
  [ "$(fold_axis 5 40)"   = "40 iv" ]    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (iv 40 > inst 5 must win as iv)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$(fold_axis 40 5)"   = "40 inst" ]  || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (inst 40 > iv 5 must win as inst)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$(fold_axis 40 na)"  = "40 inst" ]  || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (an na iv must degrade to inst)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$(fold_axis 7 7)"    = "7 inst" ]   || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a tie must resolve to inst)"; exit 1; }

  # 2. the channel set is exactly the declared width, and a foreign channel cannot enter the sum.
  #    ch2 in this fixture carries a huge value: if it were summed, beats would be 3000000, not 300.
  cat >"$td/perf.csv" <<'EOF'
100,,amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_0/,2000000000,100.00,,
200,,amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_1/,2000000000,100.00,,
2999700,,amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_2/,2000000000,100.00,,
EOF
  got="$(sum_beats <"$td/perf.csv")" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (sum_beats rejected a well-formed fixture)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$got" = 300 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (sum must be ch0+ch1=300, got $got — a foreign channel entered the total)"; exit 1; }
  printf '100,,amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_0/,2000000000,100.00,,\n' >"$td/half.csv"
  sum_beats <"$td/half.csv" >/dev/null 2>&1 && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a missing declared channel must fail, not half-report)"; exit 1; }

  # 3. classify is calibrated against the measured corpus, and UNRATED when the ceiling is unknown
  [ "$(classify 92)" = SATURATED ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (92% must be SATURATED)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$(classify 67)" = BUSY ]      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (67% must be BUSY)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$(classify 12)" = LIGHT ]     || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (12% must be LIGHT)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$(classify 3)"  = IDLE ]      || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (3% must be IDLE)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$(classify na)" = UNRATED ]   || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (an underived ceiling must be UNRATED, never a rated 0)"; exit 1; }

  # 4. the interval lane: coverage is COMPUTED from the rows, and a stale sampler renders na (not 0)
  _now="$(date +%s)"
  : > "$td/state"; printf 'cursor=%s\n' "$((_now-100))" >> "$td/state"
  : > "$td/samples"
  for i in $(seq 1 50); do printf '%d\t1.500\n' "$((_now-100+i))" >> "$td/samples"; done
  read -r _m _p _c _n <<<"$(STATE="$td/state" SAMPLES="$td/samples" bash -c 'STATE="'"$td/state"'"; SAMPLES="'"$td/samples"'"; SAMPLER_LEASE=120; SAMPLER_PERIOD=1; '"$(declare -f interval_from_sampler)"'; interval_from_sampler')"
  [ "$_c" = 50 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (50 rows over a 100 s span must report 50% coverage, got $_c)"; exit 1; }
  [ "$_m" = 1.50 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (interval mean must be 1.50, got $_m)"; exit 1; }
  # duplicate rows (two samplers on one log) must not mint coverage: 100 rows over a 100 s span with
  # every timestamp doubled is still 50 distinct seconds, i.e. 50% — not 100%.
  cp "$td/samples" "$td/dup"; cat "$td/samples" >> "$td/dup"
  read -r _md _pd _cd _nd <<<"$(bash -c 'STATE="'"$td/state"'"; SAMPLES="'"$td/dup"'"; SAMPLER_LEASE=120; SAMPLER_PERIOD=1; '"$(declare -f interval_from_sampler)"'; interval_from_sampler')"
  [ "$_cd" = 50 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (duplicated rows minted coverage: 50 distinct seconds reported as $_cd%)"; exit 1; }
  : > "$td/stale"; printf '%d\t9.000\n' "$((_now-9999))" >> "$td/stale"
  read -r _m2 _p2 _c2 _n2 <<<"$(bash -c 'STATE="'"$td/state"'"; SAMPLES="'"$td/stale"'"; SAMPLER_LEASE=120; SAMPLER_PERIOD=1; '"$(declare -f interval_from_sampler)"'; interval_from_sampler')"
  [ "$_m2" = na ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a sampler stale past its lease must render na, got $_m2)"; exit 1; }

  # 5. THE GATE, DRIVEN RED. Two ways, because each covers what the other cannot.
  #    5a. perf itself failing (the unloaded-module error is rc=129 with "Cannot find PMU") must
  #        render `na` with a reason and exit 2 — never a 0.00 GB/s reading.
  cat >"$td/perf-stub" <<'EOF'
#!/bin/sh
echo "event syntax error: 'amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_0/'" >&2
echo "Cannot find PMU \`amd_df'. Missing kernel support?" >&2
exit 129
EOF
  chmod +x "$td/perf-stub"
  out="$(MESH_DRAM_PERF="$td/perf-stub" MESH_DRAM_SUDO= "$0" 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 2 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a perf that errors must exit 2, got rc=$rc: $out)"; exit 1; }
  case "$out" in *na*reason=perf-rc=129*) :;; *) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a failing perf must render na with its reason, got: $out)"; exit 1;; esac
  case "$out" in *0.00*) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a failing perf rendered a 0.00 reading: $out)"; exit 1;; esac
  #    5b. a perf that succeeds but reports a ZERO delta is the other half of the gate — a counter
  #        that is present and dead must not read as a calm 0 GB/s.
  cat >"$td/perf-zero" <<'EOF'
#!/bin/sh
echo "0,,amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_0/,2000000000,100.00,,"
echo "0,,amd_df/dram_channel_data_controller_1/,2000000000,100.00,,"
exit 0
EOF
  chmod +x "$td/perf-zero"
  out="$(MESH_DRAM_PERF="$td/perf-zero" MESH_DRAM_SUDO= "$0" 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 2 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a zero delta must exit 2, got rc=$rc: $out)"; exit 1; }
  case "$out" in *reason=zero-delta*) :;; *) echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a zero delta must say so, got: $out)"; exit 1;; esac

  # 6. sandbox honesty — nothing above may have touched the live artifacts
  _sig2="$(stat -c '%y %s' "$_live_state" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)|$(stat -c '%y %s' "$_live_log" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)"
  [ "$_sig2" = "$_sig" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (--test stamped the LIVE artifacts: '$_sig' → '$_sig2')"; exit 1; }

  # 7. LIVE legs — the organ itself. Absent hardware is an honest n/a (exit 2), never a fail.
  grep -qi 'AuthenticAMD' /proc/cpuinfo 2>/dev/null || { echo "smoke-test: n/a (not an AMD node — no Data Fabric uncore PMU)"; exit 2; }
  sudo -n true >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: n/a (no passwordless sudo — perf_event_paranoid=4 needs root to read uncore)"; exit 2; }
  _was_loaded=0; [ -d "$SYSFS_DF" ] && _was_loaded=1
  arm || { echo "smoke-test: n/a (amd_df absent and modprobe amd_uncore failed)"; exit 2; }
  live="$(MESH_DRAM_SAMPLE_SECS=1 "$0" --json 2>&1)"; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 0 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (live read failed rc=$rc: $live)"; exit 1; }
  printf '%s\n' "$live" | grep -qE '"gbps":[0-9]+\.[0-9]+' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (live read produced no gbps: $live)"; exit 1; }
  printf '%s\n' "$live" | grep -q '"gbps":0.00' && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (live read produced 0.00 GB/s — a live node always moves some DRAM)"; exit 1; }

  # 8. THE REAL KERNEL STATE, BOTH DIRECTIONS — unload amd_uncore and drive the tool twice.
  #    The first version of this leg asserted "unloaded → red" and went GREEN-then-FAIL for the right
  #    reason: a SELF-ARMING tool re-modprobes and reads fine, so "unloaded" is not the failure mode
  #    at all. What actually fails is unloaded AND unable to arm (a uid-1000 caller: no sudo to
  #    modprobe, and perf_event_paranoid=4 refuses the read anyway). So this leg asserts both:
  #      · unloaded + unprivileged → na, rc 2, never a 0.00 calm
  #      · unloaded + privileged   → SELF-ARMS and reads, and amd_df is present afterwards
  #    rmmod refuses while any perf event is open (a running sampler holds one), which is the
  #    interlock: a busy module SKIPS this leg LOUDLY rather than fighting a live reader.
  _red=SKIPPED
  if sudo -n modprobe -r amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    [ -d "$SYSFS_DF" ] && { sudo -n modprobe amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (modprobe -r reported success but amd_df is still present — the unload leg would assert nothing)"; exit 1; }
    out2="$(MESH_DRAM_SUDO= MESH_DRAM_SAMPLE_SECS=1 "$0" 2>&1)"; rc2=$?
    if [ "$rc2" != 2 ]; then sudo -n modprobe amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unloaded + unprivileged must exit 2, got rc=$rc2: $out2)"; exit 1; fi
    case "$out2" in *na*unarmed*) :;; *) sudo -n modprobe amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unloaded + unprivileged must render na unarmed, got: $out2)"; exit 1;; esac
    case "$out2" in *0.00*) sudo -n modprobe amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (unloaded rendered a 0.00 reading: $out2)"; exit 1;; esac
    out3="$(MESH_DRAM_SAMPLE_SECS=1 "$0" 2>&1)"; rc3=$?
    [ "$rc3" = 0 ] || { sudo -n modprobe amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (self-arm from an unloaded module failed rc=$rc3: $out3)"; exit 1; }
    [ -d "$SYSFS_DF" ] || { sudo -n modprobe amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (the run reported a reading but left no amd_df — it did not really arm)"; exit 1; }
    _red="red+self-arm verified against a really-unloaded module"
  else
    _red="SKIPPED (amd_uncore busy or absent — a live reader holds the counter; 5a/5b cover the same branch via stub)"
  fi
  # leave the module as this test found it
  [ "$_was_loaded" = 1 ] || sudo -n modprobe -r amd_uncore >/dev/null 2>&1

  # 9. THE ORPHAN LEG — the leak this tool actually caused: a killed sampler leaving a ROOT perf
  #    holding DF counters forever. Drive a real sampler, kill its wrapper, assert nothing survives.
  #    Skipped LOUDLY (never silently) while a production sampler holds the lock, because the reaper
  #    cannot distinguish another sampler's perf from a stranded one and would reap the live organ.
  _orph=SKIPPED
  if command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 && flock -n "$MESH/.dram-bw-sampler.lock" true 2>/dev/null; then
    MESH_DIR="$td" MESH_DRAM_SAMPLES="$td/s.log" "$0" --sampler >/dev/null 2>&1 &
    _sw=$!
    for _i in $(seq 1 20); do sleep 0.5; [ -n "$(perf_sessions)" ] && break; done
    [ -n "$(perf_sessions)" ] || { kill "$_sw" 2>/dev/null; echo "smoke-test: FAIL (the sampler opened no perf session at all)"; exit 1; }
    sleep 3   # the session appears within ~0.3 s; rows only start at the first 1 Hz tick
    kill "$_sw" 2>/dev/null; sleep 3
    if [ -n "$(perf_sessions)" ]; then
      reap_orphans
      echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a killed sampler stranded its ROOT perf — the DF counters leak, refcnt never drops)"; exit 1
    fi
    [ -s "$td/s.log" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (the sampler wrote no rows before it was killed)"; exit 1; }
    _orph="reaped (real sampler killed, no root perf survived, $(wc -l < "$td/s.log") rows written)"
  else
    _orph="SKIPPED (a production sampler holds the lock — reaping it would kill the live organ)"
  fi

  _sig3="$(stat -c '%y %s' "$_live_state" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)|$(stat -c '%y %s' "$_live_log" 2>/dev/null || echo absent)"
  [ "$_sig3" = "$_sig" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (the live legs stamped the LIVE artifacts)"; exit 1; }
  echo "smoke-test: ok (fold + channel-width + calibration classify + coverage/lease fixtures; live uncore read; gate red via stub rc=129 and zero-delta; unload leg: $_red; orphan leg: $_orph)"
  exit 0
fi

# ── run ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
JSON=0
for a in "$@"; do
  case "$a" in
    --json)    JSON=1 ;;
    --sampler) run_sampler; exit 0 ;;
  esac
done

TS="$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
na() {   # $1 = reason
  if [ "$JSON" = 1 ]; then
    printf '{"level":"na","gbps":null,"reason":"%s","ts":"%s"}\n' "$1" "$TS"
  else
    printf '[dram-bw] na — reason=%s (no reading; the counter is not a calm 0) @ %s\n' "$1" "$TS"
  fi
  exit 2
}

arm || na "unarmed:modprobe-amd_uncore-failed"

reason="$( { inst="$(sample_gbps "$SAMPLE_SECS")"; } 2>&1 1>/dev/null )"
inst="$(sample_gbps "$SAMPLE_SECS" 2>/dev/null)" || na "$reason"
[ -n "$inst" ] || na "${reason:-empty-read}"

read -r iv_mean iv_peak iv_cov iv_rows <<<"$(interval_from_sampler)"
read -r fold_val fold_win <<<"$(fold_axis "$inst" "$iv_mean")"

peak="$(peak_gbps)"
if [ "$peak" = na ]; then
  pct=na
else
  pct="$(awk -v v="$fold_val" -v p="$peak" 'BEGIN{ printf "%.1f", v*100/p }')"
fi
level="$(classify "$pct")"

if [ "$iv_mean" = na ]; then
  cov="inst-only(${SAMPLE_SECS}s)"
else
  cov="${iv_cov}%(${iv_rows}rows)"
fi

# state: the cursor for the next interval delta + the reading. mtime is LIVENESS (the tool ran),
# content is the value — decoupled on purpose (liveness-touch convention).
{
  printf 'cursor=%s\n' "$(date +%s)"
  printf 'level=%s|gbps=%s|window=%s|inst=%s|iv=%s|iv_peak=%s|cov=%s|peak=%s|pct=%s|ch=%s|ts=%s\n' \
    "$level" "$fold_val" "$fold_win" "$inst" "$iv_mean" "$iv_peak" "$cov" "$peak" "$pct" "$(echo $DRAM_CHANNELS | tr ' ' '+')" "$TS"
} > "$STATE"
command -v mesh-state-touch >/dev/null 2>&1 && mesh-state-touch "$STATE" >/dev/null 2>&1

prev="$(awk -F'|' '/^level=/{split($1,a,"="); print a[2]}' "$STATE.prev" 2>/dev/null | tail -1)"
if [ "$level" != "${prev:-}" ]; then
  printf '%s %s gbps=%s window=%s inst=%s iv=%s iv_peak=%s cov=%s pct=%s\n' \
    "$TS" "$level" "$fold_val" "$fold_win" "$inst" "$iv_mean" "$iv_peak" "$cov" "$pct" >> "$LOG"
  grep '^level=' "$STATE" > "$STATE.prev" 2>/dev/null
fi

if [ "$JSON" = 1 ]; then
  printf '{"level":"%s","gbps":%s,"window":"%s","inst_gbps":%s,"iv_gbps":%s,"iv_peak_gbps":%s,"coverage":"%s","peak_gbps":%s,"pct_of_peak":%s,"channels":"%s","bytes_per_beat":%s,"ts":"%s"}\n' \
    "$level" "$fold_val" "$fold_win" "$inst" \
    "$( [ "$iv_mean" = na ] && echo null || echo "$iv_mean" )" \
    "$( [ "$iv_peak" = na ] && echo null || echo "$iv_peak" )" \
    "$cov" "$( [ "$peak" = na ] && echo null || echo "$peak" )" \
    "$( [ "$pct" = na ] && echo null || echo "$pct" )" \
    "$(echo $DRAM_CHANNELS | tr ' ' '+')" "$BYTES_PER_BEAT" "$TS"
else
  printf '[dram-bw] %s — %s GB/s (%s%% of %s peak) window=%s inst=%s iv=%s iv_peak=%s cov=%s ch=%s @ %s\n' \
    "$level" "$fold_val" "$pct" "$peak" "$fold_win" "$inst" "$iv_mean" "$iv_peak" "$cov" \
    "$(echo $DRAM_CHANNELS | tr ' ' '+')" "$TS"
fi
